 "literal": "Like a leather skin spread out, in the vast white saline wasteland, / like a deer chased by a man, / running and escaping is indeed possible, / but the life with relatives hinders the leg, alas!",
 "translation": "Like a deer chased by a hunter in a vast, white saline wasteland that looks like a spread-out hide, one could easily run and escape. But alas, a life bound to one's kin restrains the feet.",
 "modern": "பரப்பி வைக்கப்பட்ட தோலைப் போன்ற பெரிய வெண்மையான களர் நிலத்தில், ஒருவனால் துரத்தப்படும் மானைப் போல, ஓடித் தப்பிப் பிழைப்பது சாத்தியம்தான். ஆனால், சுற்றத்தாருடன் கூடி வாழும் வாழ்க்கையானது கால்களைத் தடுத்து நிறுத்துகிறது.",
 "explanation": "The poet Oreruzhavar reflects on the tension between personal freedom and familial duty in this Porunmozhi Kanchi poem. He compares a struggling individual to a deer being chased by a hunter across a barren, wide-open saline desert. While it is physically possible to run away and escape the hardships of life, the attachment to one's dependent relatives acts as a shackle. The vivid image of the saline wasteland looking like a stretched-out hide emphasizes the harshness and exposure of the world. Ultimately, the poem highlights how love and responsibility for one's kin prevent a person from simply fleeing their troubles.",
